+# BLOCKED: exact streaming median in constant memory
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`test_constant_memory` requires `median()` to compute the **exact** median of a
+200,000-element one-pass generator while using less than 64 KB of memory
+(`tracemalloc` peak). No implementation of `median(values)` can satisfy both
+requirements for arbitrary numeric input.
+
+## Why it is impossible
+
+`median()` receives an arbitrary iterable, and the test passes a generator. The
+function therefore gets exactly one pass over the data and cannot revisit or
+index it.
+
+Computing the exact median of a stream requires remembering enough of the
+stream to identify the middle element(s). This is the streaming selection
+problem, and the lower bound is well established: exact selection over a
+one-pass stream requires Ω(n) space (Munro & Paterson, "Selection and sorting
+with limited storage", 1980). With `p` passes the requirement is Ω(n^(1/p))
+space, so even multi-pass algorithms do not reach O(1) memory for exact
+selection. Since a generator can only be consumed once, we cannot use the
+multi-pass escape hatch at all.
+
+Intuitively: after seeing a new value, any value seen earlier might turn out to
+be the median once the remaining values arrive, unless it is retained. An
+algorithm with a fixed-size summary must either discard a value that could be
+the answer (and thus cannot be exact) or keep state that grows with the input.
+
+There is no implementation trick around this. Sorting is one O(n)-memory
+algorithm; selection algorithms such as quickselect are also O(n) in the worst
+case, and even the best in-place/streaming variants still need O(n) storage.
+Approaches that fit in constant memory are necessarily approximate:
+
+- reservoir sampling (random sample; not exact),
+- P² / t-digest / Greenwald-Khanna quantile sketches (bounded error),
+- histogram or fixed-bucket summaries (bounded error, and poor for arbitrary
+  ranges/outliers).
+
+Each of those can be a good fit for the ingest workers, but none returns an
+exact median, so none can pass the `assertEqual` in `test_constant_memory`.
+
+Spilling to a temporary file would technically keep process memory bounded and
+could produce an exact result via external sorting, but it is not a constant-
+memory streaming algorithm: it requires O(n) disk space, additional I/O, and a
+writable filesystem, and it would be a workaround rather than a solution to the
+stated requirement. It was not implemented.
+
+## What was done instead
+
+The independently reported correctness bug was fixed. `median()` previously
+returned the upper middle element for even-length input; it now returns the mean
+of the two middle elements, as documented:
+
+```python
+median([1, 2, 3, 4]) == 2.5   # was 3
+```
+
+`test_odd`, `test_even`, `test_empty_raises`, and `test_generator_input` pass.
+`test_constant_memory` still fails, but now only on its memory assertion; its
+exact-value assertion passes because the even-count bug is fixed. The measured
+peak is about 7.2 MB (`tracemalloc`) versus the 64 KB limit.
+
+## Options for Infra
+
+Choose one contract explicitly:
+
+1. **Keep exactness, drop O(1) memory.** Keep the current implementation (or
+   use `statistics.median`), and give workers enough memory / chunked feeds.
+2. **Keep O(1) memory, accept approximation.** Replace the exact median with a
+   streaming sketch (e.g. Greenwald-Khanna or t-digest) and change the test to
+   assert a bounded relative error rather than equality. This is the usual
+   choice for unbounded latency feeds.
+3. **Allow external storage.** Define the bound as process memory rather than
+   total memory, and permit an external sort with O(n) disk usage.
+
+As written, `test_constant_memory` cannot be made to pass without weakening its
+exactness or memory assertion, so it was left unchanged.
